RUDRAM_INTRO = "Sri Rudram is from the Krishna Yajur Veda (Taittiriya Samhita 4.5 and 4.7). The Namakam, named for its repeated 'namo', has 11 anuvakas (sections) that salute Rudra in every form — fierce and gentle, in all places, people and things — and ask him to turn his weapons away. The Chamakam, named for its repeated 'cha me' ('and to me'), has 11 anuvakas asking for everything a full life needs. It is traditionally learned by ear from a teacher, with svaras (Vedic accents); use this text to understand and memorise, and learn the chanting from a guru or a trusted recording."

ANUVAKA_THEMES = {
 1: "Pacifying Rudra: salutations to his anger, bow and arrows, and a prayer that his weapons become gentle and are turned away.",
 2: "Rudra as lord of all: of the directions, trees, animals, paths, fields, forests, food and the healthy.",
 3: "Rudra in all people, even thieves and robbers: lord of stealers, cheats, night-prowlers and those who carry weapons.",
 4: "Rudra in every group and craft: hosts, troops, chariot-makers, potters, smiths, hunters, dogs and their keepers.",
 5: "The famous 'namaH shivAya' anuvaka core begins here: Bhava, Rudra, Sharva, Pashupati, the blue-throated one, and Rudra in waters and islands.",
 6: "Rudra in all ages and stages: eldest and youngest, born earlier and later, in fields, forests, echoes, and as the warrior.",
 7: "Rudra in sounds, weapons and all waters: drums, messengers, streams, wells, rain, clouds and lightning.",
 8: "The heart of Rudram: 'namaH shivAya cha shivatarAya cha' — salutations to Shiva and the more auspicious. Rudra on both banks of every river.",
 9: "Rudra in every place: barren land, paths, homes, beds, caves, dust, dry and green things, and in the hearts of the gods.",
 10: "Prayers for protection: do not harm our children, elders, cattle or heroes; lay down your weapons and come with your bow unstrung.",
 11: "Salutations to the countless Rudras on earth, in the sky and heaven, followed by the concluding mantras including the Mahamrityunjaya.",
}

NAMAKAM = {
 (1,1): "Salutations to your anger, O Rudra, and to your arrow. Salutations to your bow and to both your arms.",
 (1,2): "Your arrow that is most auspicious, your bow that has become auspicious, and your quiver that is auspicious — with these, O Rudra, make us happy.",
 (1,3): "O Rudra, dweller on the mountain who brings peace, look upon us with your most blissful form, which is auspicious, not fearsome, and reveals only good.",
 (1,4): "O dweller on the mountain, make gentle the arrow you hold in your hand to shoot. O protector of the mountain, do not harm humans or the world.",
 (1,5): "O dweller on the mountain, we praise you with auspicious words, so that our whole world may be free of disease and of good mind.",
 (1,6): "May he, the first divine physician, speak on our behalf. Crushing all serpents, may he drive away all evil spirits.",
 (1,7): "He who is copper-coloured, ruddy, brown and most auspicious — and the thousands of Rudras who surround him in all directions — we seek to turn away their anger.",
 (1,8): "He who moves along, blue-throated and red — the cowherds have seen him, the water-carrying women have seen him, all beings have seen him. May he, being seen, make us happy.",
 (1,9): "Salutations to the blue-throated, thousand-eyed giver of bounty. To his attendants too I offer my salutations.",
 (1,10): "O Lord, loosen the string from both ends of your bow, and lay aside the arrows that are in your hand.",
 (1,11): "O thousand-eyed one with a hundred quivers, unstringing your bow and blunting the tips of your arrows, become gracious and kindly towards us.",
 (1,12): "May the bow of the one with matted hair be unstrung, his quiver empty of arrows, his arrows powerless, and his sheath empty.",
 (1,13): "O most bountiful one, with the weapon in your hand and your bow, protect us on all sides, free from disease.",
 (1,14): "Salutations to your weapon, not yet drawn but powerful. Salutations to both your arms and to your bow.",
 (1,15): "May your weapon pass us by on every side, and may your quiver be kept far away from us.",
 (1,16): "Salutations to you, O Lord: lord of the universe, the great god, three-eyed, destroyer of the three cities, the fire of the three fires, the fire of dissolution, blue-throated, conqueror of death, lord of all, ever-auspicious, the glorious great god — salutations.",
 (2,1): "Salutations to the golden-armed commander and lord of the directions; to the trees with green leaves and the lord of animals; to the radiant, tawny one and lord of paths; to the brown-hued piercer and lord of food; to the green-haired one wearing the sacred thread and lord of the well-nourished; to the weapon of Bhava and lord of the worlds; to Rudra with drawn bow and lord of fields; to the charioteer who cannot be harmed and lord of forests.",
 (2,2): "Salutations to the red one, the master builder and lord of trees; to the counsellor and trader, lord of thickets; to the creator of the world who is generous to devotees, lord of herbs; to the one with the loud war-cry who makes enemies weep, lord of foot-soldiers; to the one fully armoured and running, lord of the brave.",
 (3,1): "Salutations to the one who overpowers and pierces, lord of the attacking armies; to the excellent one with a sword, lord of thieves; to the one with sword and quiver, lord of robbers; to the cheat and the great deceiver, lord of pilferers; to the one who moves about stealthily, lord of forests; to those armed and intent on killing, lord of plunderers; to those with swords who roam at night, lord of cutthroats; to the turbaned mountain-dweller, lord of land-grabbers.",
 (3,2): "Salutations to you who bear arrows and bows; to you who string the bow and fit the arrow; to you who draw and release; to you who shoot and pierce; to you who sit and lie down; to you who sleep and wake; to you who stand and run; to you who are assemblies and their presidents; to you who are horses and their masters.",
 (4,1): "Salutations to you, the attacking and piercing female powers; to the fierce goddesses and the destructive ones; to you who are the greedy and their leaders; to the hosts and their lords; to the troops and their chiefs; to the misshapen and those of every shape; to the great and the small; to those with chariots and without.",
 (4,2): "Salutations to you who are chariots and their owners; armies and their commanders; charioteers and drivers; carpenters and chariot-makers; potters and smiths; bird-catchers and fishermen; makers of arrows and bows; hunters and dog-keepers; dogs and their masters.",
 (5,1): "Salutations to Bhava (source of creation) and Rudra; to Sharva (destroyer) and Pashupati (lord of beings); to the blue-throated one with white throat; to the one with matted locks and the one with shaven head; to the thousand-eyed one with a hundred bows; to the mountain-dweller who pervades all beings; to the most generous one who bears arrows; to the short and the dwarf; to the vast and the very old; to the aged and the one who grows with praise.",
 (5,2): "Salutations to the foremost and the first; to the swift and the moving; to the quick and the one in rushing waters; to the one in waves and in still waters; to the one in streams and in islands.",
 (6,1): "Salutations to the eldest and the youngest; to the first-born and the later-born; to the middle one and the immature; to the last-born and the one at the root; to the one between worlds and the one in charms; to the one in death and in welfare; to the one in fields and threshing floors; to the one in Vedic verses and in the end of the Vedas; to the one in forests and thickets; to sound and its echo.",
 (6,2): "Salutations to the one with swift armies and swift chariots; to the hero who breaks the enemy; to the one with armour and a protective shell; to the one with helmet and breastplate; to the renowned one with a renowned army.",
 (7,1): "Salutations to the one in drums and in drumsticks; to the bold and the reflective; to the messenger and the one sent; to the one with sword and quiver; to the one with sharp arrows and weapons; to the one with good weapons and a fine bow; to the one in roads and paths; to the one in rough ground and waterfalls; to the one in marshes and lakes; to the one in rivers and ponds.",
 (7,2): "Salutations to the one in wells and pits; in rain and in drought; in clouds and in lightning; in autumn clouds and in sunshine; in wind and in storms; to the one in dwellings and their protector.",
 (8,1): "Salutations to Soma (with Uma) and Rudra; to the copper-hued and the red one; to the bringer of happiness and Pashupati; to the fierce and the terrible; to the one who strikes from near and from far; to the slayer and the destroyer of all; to the trees with green leaves; to Tara (who carries across) — salutations to the source of happiness in this world and the next; to the giver of happiness here and hereafter; to Shiva, the auspicious, and to Shivatara, the more auspicious than all.",
 (8,2): "Salutations to the one in holy places and on river banks; to the one on the far shore and the near shore; to the one who carries us across and lifts us out; to the one at the crossing and the one in this world; to the one in the young grass and in the foam; to the one in the sand and in the current.",
 (9,1): "Salutations to the one in barren land and on wide roads; in rocky places and in habitable places; to the one with matted hair and the one in front; to the one in cowsheds and in houses; in beds and in mansions; in thorny bushes and in caves; in deep waters and in dew; in dust and in mist; in dry and in green things; in hard ground and in grassy places.",
 (9,2): "Salutations to the one on the earth and in the waves; in leaves and in fallen leaves; to the one who raises his weapon and the one who strikes; to the one who troubles a little and greatly. Salutations to you who give abundance, who are the hearts of the gods; to the indestructible ones, to those who discern, to those who give wealth, and to those who are beyond decay.",
 (10,1): "O Rudra, who drives sinners away, lord of food, possessor of nothing, blue and red — do not frighten or harm these people and these animals of ours. Let none of them fall sick.",
 (10,2): "O Rudra, your auspicious form that is always healing, auspicious and the healer of disease — with that form, make us happy so that we may live.",
 (10,3): "We offer our thoughts to this mighty Rudra with matted locks, destroyer of heroes, so that there may be well-being for our two-footed and four-footed ones, and everything in this village may be nourished and free from disease.",
 (10,4): "O Rudra, be gracious to us and bring us happiness. We worship you, destroyer of heroes, with salutations. Whatever wellness and freedom from sin our father Manu gained by worship, may we gain that under your guidance.",
 (10,5): "O Rudra, do not harm our elders or our children, our young men or the unborn in the womb, our father or our mother, nor our dear bodies.",
 (10,6): "Do not harm our children or grandchildren, our lives, our cattle or our horses. O Rudra, do not in anger slay our heroes. We worship you with offerings and salutations.",
 (10,7): "Your weapon that kills cattle and people, keep it far from us; may your favour be with us, destroyer of heroes. Protect us, O God, speak for us, and give us happiness, O lord of both worlds.",
 (10,8): "Praise the famous Rudra, seated in the heart, ever-young, fierce like a lion, the destroyer. O Rudra, being praised, be gracious to the one who praises you; may your armies strike others, not us.",
 (10,9): "May Rudra's weapon pass us by, and may the wrath of the fierce one turn away from us. Unstring your strong bow for the sake of those who make offerings; O bountiful one, be gracious to our children and grandchildren.",
 (10,10): "O most bountiful, most auspicious one, be auspicious and kindly towards us. Placing your weapon on the highest tree, wearing the hide, come to us carrying only your bow, Pinaka.",
 (10,11): "O remover of troubles, O red one, salutations to you, O Lord. May your thousand weapons strike others than us.",
 (10,12): "Thousands upon thousands of weapons are in your arms. O Lord, you who are their master, turn their points away from us.",
 (11,1): "The thousands of Rudras who are upon the earth — we unstring their bows a thousand yojanas away.",
 (11,2): "The Rudras who dwell in this great ocean of the mid-air.",
 (11,3): "The blue-necked, white-throated Sharvas who move below on the earth.",
 (11,4): "The blue-necked, white-throated Rudras who dwell in heaven.",
 (11,5): "Those in the trees, green as young grass, blue-necked and red.",
 (11,6): "Those who are lords of beings, without tufts of hair or with matted locks.",
 (11,7): "Those who strike people in their food and those who drink from vessels.",
 (11,8): "Those who guard the paths, who bring food, who fight battles.",
 (11,9): "Those who roam holy places with spears and swords.",
 (11,10): "These and the many more Rudras spread in all directions — we unstring their bows a thousand yojanas away.",
 (11,11): "Salutations to the Rudras on the earth, in the mid-air and in heaven, whose arrows are food, wind and rain. To them I offer ten fingers facing east, ten south, ten west, ten north, ten upward — salutations to them. May they make us happy. The one we hate and who hates us, I place in your jaws.",
}

